<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Mirandola</h1><p class="st-meta">1311 CE – 1710 CE (approx.) · capital Mirandola</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The small Emilian state of the Pico family, birthplace of the philosopher Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, which lasted four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-pico">Pico</a> family held lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, and in 1311 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-pico">Francesco Pico</a> was named imperial vicar there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VII</a>. The family ruled as lords and imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and the state was raised to a principality in 1596 and a duchy in 1617.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-pico">Pico</a> governed a fortified town and the countryside of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> and Concordia, often allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The humanist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a>, author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oration-on-the-dignity-of-man">Oration on the Dignity of Man</a>, was a younger son of the family. In 1511 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> personally led the siege of the fortress. Family quarrels and murders were frequent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-pico">Francesco Maria Pico</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> and was deposed by the emperor in 1708. In 1710 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rinaldo-d-este-duke-of-modena">Rinaldo d'Este</a>, joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-mirandola.jpg" alt="Duchy of Mirandola" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Johann Carl Hemeling · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_Mirandola_by_J._C._Hemeling_(1732).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1311 CE – 1710 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mirandola</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francesco Pico, Galeotto I Pico, Francesco Maria Pico</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lordship of Mirandola, Mirandola and Concordia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1510&amp;focus=duchy-of-mirandola" aria-label="Duchy of Mirandola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4944 592 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Mirandola lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5264 848L5229 694M5264 848L5261 855"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5261 855h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5264 848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5264 848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mirandola. <a href="/atlas?year=1510&amp;focus=duchy-of-mirandola">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
